# karatep: turn on Qualcomm Fluence dual-mic noise suppression for capture.
#
# The hardware has two internal analog mics -- AMIC1 (primary, bottom) and
# AMIC3 (secondary) -- and ro.vendor.audio.sdk.fluencetype=fluence already
# advertises FLUENCE_DUAL_MIC. The vendor shipped every persist switch off,
# so capture used the single-mic snd_device (68: handset-mic) and the second
# mic sat idle.
#
# Measured on hardware, 8 s of speech over deliberate background noise,
# same acoustic conditions, only this property changed:
#
#             snd_device            speech     noise floor    SNR
#   off       68: handset-mic       -27.7 dBFS   -42.4 dBFS   14.7 dB
#   on        73: dmic-endfire      -25.8 dBFS   -61.8 dBFS   36.0 dB
#
# Speech came out 1.9 dB *louder* while the floor dropped 19.4 dB, so this is
# real dual-mic suppression and not just a quieter input path. The dual-mic
# ACDB calibration is present and healthy: a 2-channel capture selects
# 115: handset-stereo-dmic-ef and returns two independent mic signals
# (high-frequency L/R correlation 0.05).
#
# post-fs-data, not post-fs: persistent properties are loaded from /data
# during post-fs-data, and setting a persist.* property before that gets
# overwritten by the stored value.
#
# Only the two capture switches are set here. persist.vendor.audio.fluence.
# voicecall and .speaker drive in-call and speakerphone routing, which cannot
# be verified on this device yet -- there is only a dummy SIM -- so they are
# deliberately left alone until a real call can be tested.
on post-fs-data
    setprop persist.vendor.audio.fluence.audiorec true
    setprop persist.vendor.audio.fluence.voicerec true
